                <pb facs="0918" n="814" sameAs="frus:frus1913/pg814"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/8113.</note>
                <head ana="#head_untitled-inferred-from-preceding-sibling-head">[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1913-07-22T21:00:00-06:36">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 22, 1913—9 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>354. I did not attend the demonstration in honor of the Japanese Minister today,
                    as I feared it would be made the occasion of an anti-American demonstration. One
                    speech of a rather anti-American nature was made, but with this exception no
                    anti-American expression was allowed although the demonstration was meant as one
                    against the United States rather than as a greeting to the new Japanese
                    Minister. The police had strict orders to immediately suppress any anti-American
                    expression.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nelson O’Shaughnessy</hi>.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
